WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.800
DBSCAN.

2
00:00:02.100 --> 00:00:06.000
A clustering method that needs no k, and knows what an outlier is.

3
00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:11.333
DBSCAN clusters by density, not by distance to a centroid.

4
00:00:11.633 --> 00:00:13.067
There is no k to choose.

5
00:00:13.367 --> 00:00:17.700
And points in sparse regions are labeled noise, with the label minus one.

6
00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:20.133
Here is how it works.

7
00:00:20.433 --> 00:00:23.967
For each point, count its neighbors within a radius called eps.

8
00:00:24.267 --> 00:00:27.067
If there are at least min samples, it is a core point.

9
00:00:27.367 --> 00:00:30.000
Core points within reach of each other chain together.

10
00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:32.933
And the chain becomes a cluster, whatever its shape.

11
00:00:34.133 --> 00:00:36.000
Let's see it on two crescent moons.

12
00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:39.700
Density chains follow each curve, so each moon stays whole.

13
00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:42.269
The three isolated points join nothing.

14
00:00:42.269 --> 00:00:45.933
They are declared noise instead of being forced into a cluster.

15
00:00:47.133 --> 00:00:48.433
So how does it compare?

16
00:00:48.733 --> 00:00:50.281
K-Means asks for k.

17
00:00:50.281 --> 00:00:53.867
DBSCAN asks for eps and min samples instead.

18
00:00:54.167 --> 00:00:58.167
K-Means likes round blobs, while DBSCAN follows any shape.

19
00:00:58.467 --> 00:01:03.767
And K-Means forces every point into a cluster, but DBSCAN admits what is noise.

20
00:01:04.067 --> 00:01:07.600
The cost is that one eps struggles when densities differ a lot.

21
00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:10.367
Here it is in scikit-learn.

22
00:01:10.667 --> 00:01:14.333
We set the radius to zero point two, and need five neighbors.

23
00:01:14.633 --> 00:01:18.933
Then we count clusters, leaving out the noise label, and count the noise points.

24
00:01:20.133 --> 00:01:21.500
Here is a clever use.

25
00:01:21.800 --> 00:01:27.067
Satellite telemetry forms dense blobs for healthy modes, like downlink or eclipse.

26
00:01:27.367 --> 00:01:29.635
Run DBSCAN and ignore the clusters.

27
00:01:29.635 --> 00:01:32.033
The noise points are the deliverable.

28
00:01:32.333 --> 00:01:36.867
A reading too sparse for any known mode is exactly what an anomaly looks like.

29
00:01:38.067 --> 00:01:38.965
Let's recap.

30
00:01:38.965 --> 00:01:42.033
DBSCAN clusters by density, in any shape.

31
00:01:42.333 --> 00:01:44.767
No k, but you tune eps and min samples.

32
00:01:45.067 --> 00:01:48.500
Sparse points are noise, and that is your anomaly detector.

33
00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:50.933
Next up: hierarchical clustering.
